{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>En aquel tiempo.</b> Al cabo de los cuarenta días que mencionó, se reconcilió con él y dijo: ״פסל לך״ — labra para ti." "<b>Y te harás un arca de madera.</b> 'Y haré primero el arca, y después ״ואפסל שני לוחות אבנים״ — y labraré dos tablas de piedra,' para que tuviera un lugar listo donde ponerlas cuando descendiera con las tablas; pues si no hago primero el arca, ¿dónde las pondría? Y nuestros Sabios de bendita memoria recibieron concerniente a este arca que es el arca que Moshé hizo, no el arca que Bezalel hizo en la obra del Tabernáculo. Este arca que Moshé hizo era temporal, para que estuviera lista para poner las tablas en ella inmediatamente cuando descendiera del monte, hasta que el arca de Bezalel fuera hecha — aquella fue hecha para todas las generaciones. Esta, la de Moshé, solía salir con ellos a la guerra; aquella, la de Bezalel, no la sacaban en absoluto — solo en los días de Yehoshúa la sacaron por palabra divina, y cuando la sacaron por propia iniciativa en los días de Elí, contra los filisteos, fueron castigados por ello y fue capturada; y a causa de ella los filisteos temblaron y dijeron (I Shmuel 4:8): ״מי יצילנו מיד האלהים האדירים האלה״ — '¿Quién nos salvará de la mano de estos Elohim poderosos,' ״כי לא היתה כזאת תמול שלשום״. En este arca de Moshé se guardaban las tablas y los fragmentos de las tablas hasta que fue hecho el arca de Bezalel, en la cual colocó solo las segundas tablas; como está escrito (Shemot 25:16): ״ונתת אל הארון את העדות אשר אתן אליך״. Y lo que dijeron en Bava Batra, capítulo uno — 'no había nada en el arca excepto las dos tablas' y así sucesivamente — no es una exclusión tras una exclusión, sino más bien una inclusión: los fragmentos de las tablas también se guardaban en el arca. Esto fue después de que el Templo fue construido, pues en el Templo tanto las tablas como los fragmentos de las tablas se guardaban en el arca que Bezalel hizo." "<b>Y ya</b> he escrito en la sección de ויהי בשלח que this ark that Bezalel made was hidden away there in the Temple together with the flask of manna and the other things, and King Josiah (Yeshayahu) ordered them hidden beneath the tunnels of the Sanctuary. And the reason Josiah hid the ark more than all the other sacred vessels was for two reasons: first, for the exaltation of its holiness — for there is nothing among all the vessels as consecrated and exalted as it; and just as it precedes them all in the order of its making, so is it more consecrated than them all, for it is a likeness of the Throne of Glory and a dwelling for the Shekhinah, and the Shekhinah clung to the ark forever. Therefore in the Second Temple there was no ark, and when the ark was lacking, the Shekhinah was lacking. And since Josiah knew that the Temple was destined to be destroyed by Nebuchadnezzar, and the prophets of his generation were prophesying and crying out thus, and it was clear to him that all the vessels would be exiled to Babylon — therefore he had pity on the honor of the ark for its exaltation and wanted to hide it away. The second reason: he saw in the ark a sign of journeying — namely, that the staves which had lengthened in the days of Solomon became shortened in his days and returned to the same measure they had been in the days of Moses. And when Israel would enter the Sanctuary and see the curtain and the two staves of the ark, their heads were visible from outside the curtain like two breasts of a woman seen beneath her garments, and concerning these two staves Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Song of Songs 4:5): ״שני שדיך כשני עפרים״ and so on. And now when they became shortened in the days of Josiah and turned inward, Israel would enter and not see them protruding from the curtain as was customary — because they had returned to the measure they had in the days of Moses. And Josiah recognized from this that the ark was ready for journeying and was contracting inward. Furthermore, he saw that when Hilkiah the priest entered to perform service in the Sanctuary, he found there a Torah scroll that had come out from beside the ark of the covenant of HaShem into the Sanctuary, and he recognized from this that it was a sign for exile and for the destruction of the Temple. And this is what Targum Yonatan rendered (Ezekiel 1:1): ״ויהי בשלשים שנה״ — 'And it was in the thirtieth year,' translating: ״והוה בתלתין שנין בזמן דאשכח חלקיה כהנא רבא ספרא דאורייתא בבי מקדשא בעזרתא תחות אולמא בפלגות ליליא בתר מעלני סהרא בימי יאשיהו בן אמון.״ And since he saw these signs, he saw fit to hide it away. From this you learn that the essence of holiness belonged to this ark of Bezalel, not to the ark that Moses made temporarily. And concerning this ark of Bezalel our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Eruvin, chapter הדר: 'As long as the ark and the Shekhinah are not in their place, Israel are forbidden in conjugal relations.' For if you were to say this about the ark that Moses made, which went out with them in their wars — how is it possible to say thus, for during the seven months that the ark stayed in the field of the Philistines, would Israel have been forbidden in conjugal relations and prevented from procreation? And likewise David, who said to Uriah (II Samuel 11:8): ״רד לביתך״ — 'Go down to your house.' Rather, it was concerning the ark of Bezalel that they said this, for it is the essence of holiness — unlike the ark of Moses, whose holiness was not as great."], :book "deuteronomy", :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :es, :chapter 10, :timestamp "2026-07-30T17:17:21.362669906Z", :translated-from :en, :model "glm-5.2"}